        "id": "e1",
        "claim": "Inspected document.py, lines 31–56: overview attributes the algorithm to Lewell et al. (1998), explains hierarchical results and recommends GetLeaves. The example produces four immediate children, five descendants and three terminal fragments; leaf.mol exposes the RDKit molecule."
      },
      {
        "id": "e1",
        "claim": "Inspected RecapHierarchyNode.GetAllChildren, GetLeaves and _gacRecurse, lines 102–138: results are SMILES-keyed dictionaries; terminal nodes have no children. GetLeaves traverses children, so a root without children returns an empty dictionary."
      },
      {
        "id": "e1",
        "claim": "Inspected RecapDecompose, lines 146–213: nodes are reused by SMILES, products are recursively processed, and filters affect accepted decompositions. Positive minFragmentSize subtracts dummy atoms from explicit atom counts; default zero instead rejects fragments whose stripped SMILES are empty, C, CC or CCC."
      },
      {
        "id": "e1",
        "claim": "Inspected test1/test2, lines 223–235, and testMinFragmentSize, lines 279–293: CCCOCCC has no children under defaults but one unique leaf, *CCC, with minFragmentSize=3. CCCOCC at size 3 has no children. These assertions were read, not executed."
      },
      {
        "id": "e1",
        "claim": "Inspected imports and reactionDefs, lines 63–86: prerequisites include RDKit Chem and rdChemReactions; products contain wildcard attachment atoms. The aromatic nitrogen–aromatic carbon rule is explicitly marked outside the standard RECAP set."
      },
      {
        "id": "e2",
        "claim": "Inspected document.txt in full: BSD 3-Clause redistribution conditions require retained notices and disclaimer and restrict endorsement. document.py lines 1–29 also contain the applicable Novartis copyright notice."
      }
    ],
